1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,500
All around us, a battle is raging.

2
00:00:03,501 --> 00:00:06,500
A battle for control of the planet.

3
00:00:06,501 --> 00:00:09,500
The occupying army buzzes and skitters,

4
00:00:09,501 --> 00:00:13,500
and some of them are out for blood.

5
00:00:13,501 --> 00:00:16,500
But in this war, we have our own soldiers,

6
00:00:16,501 --> 00:00:19,000
armed with the best weapons available,

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,500
charged with stemming an onslaught of tiny adversaries.

8
00:00:23,501 --> 00:00:27,000
These warriors of the basement, garden, and field

9
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Fight for the dominance of our own species.

10
00:00:30,001 --> 00:00:34,000
Now, The Exterminator on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:54,960 --> 00:00:56,160
models.

12
00:00:56,161 --> 00:01:01,160
They're hiding in your home, under your bed, in your kitchen.

13
00:01:01,161 --> 00:01:02,160
They're everywhere.

14
00:01:02,161 --> 00:01:06,940
A female rat can drop a litter today and be pregnant again to have it tomorrow.

15
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:09,040
Cockroaches are nasty.

16
00:01:09,980 --> 00:01:11,720
Cockroaches carry diseases.

17
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:12,920
They're dirty.

18
00:01:13,020 --> 00:01:16,680
Whenever there is a sanitation issue, you will always find cockroaches.

19
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:24,660
Since man first met past, exterminators have been fighting a rogues gallery of nefarious nuisances.

20
00:01:26,220 --> 00:01:29,200
Though techniques improve as history unfolds,

21
00:01:29,201 --> 00:01:34,100
The aim has remained unchanged.

22
00:01:34,101 --> 00:01:44,400
To control a menace, stifle the aggressors, and at times, kill or be killed.

23
00:01:44,401 --> 00:01:46,400
Consider the mosquito.

24
00:01:46,401 --> 00:01:48,700
Point of contact, the skin.

25
00:01:48,701 --> 00:01:53,800
Known dangers, yellow fever, malaria, and West Nile virus.

26
00:01:53,801 --> 00:01:59,060
The mosquito is well engineered to sneak up on its prey and administer a stealthy bite.

27
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:04,380
The male mosquito only feeds on plant juices and doesn't feed that much.

28
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:08,040
The female needs a blood meal because she needs the protein to lay eggs.

29
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:13,100
When they bite, they inject a local anesthetic and an anticoagulant.

30
00:02:13,101 --> 00:02:17,280
So you don't feel them biting and it causes the blood to flow.

31
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,120
Most species ingest a full blood meal in less than a minute

32
00:02:21,121 --> 00:02:24,080
and can consume up to twice their body weight in blood.

33
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:27,680
That's about 14 ten-thousandths of an ounce.

34
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:31,260
Mosquitoes have been a pervasive and persistent pest.

35
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:38,700
In Florida, the Lee County Mosquito Control Board

36
00:02:38,701 --> 00:02:41,260
is cracking down on the winged menace from the air.

37
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:44,560
We have about a thousand square miles,

38
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:47,500
several hundred thousand acres of salt marsh areas,

39
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:49,160
and predominantly those mosquitoes,

40
00:02:49,161 --> 00:02:53,400
when they emerge from the salt marsh they can fly easily 10 to 15 miles.

41
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:57,900
Florida is host to 77 species of these biting bloodsuckers.

42
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:02,900
In the territory covered by these airborne exterminators,

43
00:03:03,460 --> 00:03:07,520
there may well be mosquitoes that are carriers or vectors of deadly microbes.

44
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:14,020
These microbes can cause blood diseases like yellow fever that result in kidney and liver failure.

45
00:03:14,021 --> 00:03:20,420
Malaria is caused by a deadly parasite that travels from the mosquito's mouth parts into the victim's bloodstream

46
00:03:20,421 --> 00:03:26,100
and can lead to kidney failure, seizures, and eventually death.

47
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:33,280
Improved medication and sanitation measures have minimized the double threat of malaria and yellow fever in the U.S.

48
00:03:35,060 --> 00:03:38,740
But another mosquito-borne disease is advancing across America.

49
00:03:38,741 --> 00:03:43,460
It's called West Nile virus, a potentially fatal brain inflammation.

50
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:46,820
People with weakened immune systems or other health problems

51
00:03:46,821 --> 00:03:50,340
have perished after a visit from the tiny angel of death.

52
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:56,480
It is carried by mosquitoes that are found in urban areas,

53
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,800
those that breed in urban-type situations.

54
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:03,660
Even a backyard birdbath or roadside puddle

55
00:04:03,661 --> 00:04:06,720
will provide harborage, as it's called by exterminators,

56
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:08,480
in which mosquitoes may breed.

57
00:04:10,120 --> 00:04:12,500
But in Florida, with its many marshes,

58
00:04:12,501 --> 00:04:15,000
mosquito breeding areas are legion.

59
00:04:15,001 --> 00:04:19,000
A helicopter comes in handy for mosquito inspectors on the move.

60
00:04:19,001 --> 00:04:22,500
We'll be going out today to do inspections in the helicopter

61
00:04:22,501 --> 00:04:25,000
looking for larvae that are developing in the field.

62
00:04:25,001 --> 00:04:27,000
If we do find a sufficient amount of larvae,

63
00:04:27,001 --> 00:04:30,000
they're going to be treated, and that's called larva sighting.

64
00:04:30,001 --> 00:04:32,000
We'll go out with an assigned pilot

65
00:04:32,001 --> 00:04:36,000
and we'll land at various locations between 20 to 30 landings

66
00:04:36,001 --> 00:04:39,000
to pinpoint the breeding areas.

67
00:04:39,001 --> 00:04:45,480
When these small game hunters spot a likely breeding area, the chopper touches down so

68
00:04:45,481 --> 00:04:48,260
the inspector can take a sample with a handheld dipper.

69
00:04:48,261 --> 00:04:54,200
Some of these areas you can get three larvae per dip, other areas you can come up with

70
00:04:54,201 --> 00:04:57,700
500 larvae per dip.

71
00:04:57,701 --> 00:05:02,600
After the inspector confirms that a significant number of mosquitoes are present, a Belljet

72
00:05:02,601 --> 00:05:09,000
Ranger helicopter fitted with a spray rig will target the area with the proper larvicides.

73
00:05:09,001 --> 00:05:13,720
the mosquitoes when they are in an immature form, before they can put the bite on humans.

74
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:17,476
We use several different types of chemicals.

75
00:05:17,477 --> 00:05:19,800
Some are organophosphates, one is abate.

76
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:23,382
Another is a bacterial product called BTI.

77
00:05:23,383 --> 00:05:26,760
The mosquito larvae have to ingest that material to

78
00:05:26,761 --> 00:05:27,862
to have it effective.

79
00:05:27,863 --> 00:05:31,640
It affects their stomach lining and other types of materials like insect

80
00:05:31,641 --> 00:05:36,360
growth regulators called altosid. If the mosquito population is on the rise,

81
00:05:36,361 --> 00:05:38,660
authorities will administer a knockout punch.

82
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:45,600
A Vietnam-era helicopter, known as a Huey,

83
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:48,740
is rigged to disperse a pesticide such as malathion

84
00:05:48,741 --> 00:05:50,420
that kills adult mosquitoes.

85
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:54,340
The pesticide is directed through the chopper exhaust system,

86
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:57,520
where the heat of the exhaust turns it into a vapor

87
00:05:57,521 --> 00:05:59,380
that can cover a wide area quickly.

88
00:06:00,380 --> 00:06:03,340
And they can carry 350 gallons of material,

89
00:06:03,341 --> 00:06:06,320
get a lot more done within a shorter period of time

90
00:06:06,321 --> 00:06:08,840
and cut down on the ferry time, which is very important to us.

91
00:06:11,860 --> 00:06:14,880
Man and mosquito have been locked in a turf war for centuries.

92
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:18,060
Early humans rubbed foul-smelling sulfur

93
00:06:18,061 --> 00:06:21,000
and even nicotine-bearing tobacco plants on their skin

94
00:06:21,001 --> 00:06:23,700
in the hope that the stench would repel pests.

95
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:26,760
They also turned to witchcraft for relief.

96
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:31,600
When it comes to herbs and spices and casting spells

97
00:06:31,601 --> 00:06:34,620
and witchcraft and all that sort of thing,

98
00:06:34,621 --> 00:06:40,880
a lot of that is truly trial and error and may take many, many generations to figure out

99
00:06:40,881 --> 00:06:43,800
whether it is in fact working or not.

100
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:52,200
As early as 450 B.C., the Greek historian Herodotus reported the use of mosquito netting over sleeping areas

101
00:06:52,201 --> 00:06:54,380
to keep mosquitoes away at night.

102
00:06:55,740 --> 00:06:59,540
In America, Indians stoked smoky fires to repel mosquitoes.

103
00:06:59,541 --> 00:07:10,380
It would not be until 1898 that a scientist named Giovanni Battista Grassi proved that

104
00:07:10,381 --> 00:07:14,660
it was the microbes found in mosquitoes that had been causing malaria and yellow fever

105
00:07:14,661 --> 00:07:15,940
for generations.

106
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:21,800
But thousands would die before practical and cost-effective methods of mosquito control

107
00:07:21,801 --> 00:07:23,440
were put into practice.

108
00:07:23,441 --> 00:07:36,053
In 1881, Ferdinand de Lesseps, builder of the Suez Canal, led a French-financed project to build a new canal, crossing the Colombian Isthmus of Panama to unite the

109
00:07:36,054 --> 00:07:38,220
Atlantic and Pacific Oceans.

110
00:07:38,640 --> 00:07:49,100
But as soon as construction started, de Lesseps found he was battling a tiny but deadly determined enemy, the indigenous mosquito whose habitat had been disturbed.

111
00:07:49,101 --> 00:07:53,100
workers soon began dying of yellow fever and malaria.

112
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:55,840
At first, no one knew why.

113
00:07:55,940 --> 00:08:00,040
Malaria is a term that really means bad air.

114
00:08:00,140 --> 00:08:04,040
And so people thought that it was coming from bad water or

115
00:08:04,140 --> 00:08:09,960
bad land or decaying vegetation from the forest floor.

116
00:08:10,060 --> 00:08:13,460
By 1884, 1,200 workers had died.

117
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:17,000
30,000 would die, including the father of the project,

118
00:08:17,001 --> 00:08:21,800
Ferdinand Alessips, before the French pulled the plug.

119
00:08:21,900 --> 00:08:24,300
In 1904, the Americans took over.

120
00:08:28,140 --> 00:08:30,240
They brought in a strong-willed medical doctor

121
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:32,440
named William Gorgas.

122
00:08:32,540 --> 00:08:36,460
Dr. William Gorgas was a very authoritative,

123
00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:41,160
almost, well, probably autocratic type of individual

124
00:08:41,260 --> 00:08:45,200
who could change the culture of the way people lived

125
00:08:45,201 --> 00:08:48,960
lived and worked in the Panama Canal zone.

126
00:08:48,961 --> 00:08:51,880
Gorgas was an evangelist for sanitation.

127
00:08:51,881 --> 00:08:53,720
His quest was driven by the very latest

128
00:08:53,721 --> 00:08:56,960
in scientific thinking, that the species of mosquito

129
00:08:56,961 --> 00:09:00,380
called anopheles, transmitted malaria.

130
00:09:00,381 --> 00:09:03,560
Gorgas brought all the tools that were available to him

131
00:09:03,561 --> 00:09:06,200
at that time, eliminating the water,

132
00:09:06,201 --> 00:09:09,560
netting the quarters in which the workers slept,

133
00:09:09,561 --> 00:09:12,260
all of the things that were necessary to cut down

134
00:09:12,261 --> 00:09:14,600
on the mosquito population.

135
00:09:14,601 --> 00:09:16,547
Gorgas' efforts were successful.

136
00:09:16,548 --> 00:09:20,380
By December 1906, he had laid waste to the mosquito population,

137
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:24,200
and yellow fever had been officially eradicated on the isthmus.

138
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:31,400
Malaria lingered on, but the number of cases was greatly diminished.

139
00:09:33,720 --> 00:09:37,540
The Panama Canal opened on August 15, 1914.

140
00:09:38,540 --> 00:09:42,660
It was a decisive victory in the battle of man versus insect.

141
00:09:42,661 --> 00:09:53,180
In the 1930s, the United States Civilian Conservation Corps drained standing water in southern states to eliminate mosquito breeding areas.

142
00:09:55,220 --> 00:10:03,600
Later in the 1940s, certain chemical compounds were dubbed pesticides because of their ability to kill pests like mosquitoes.

143
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:11,120
One of the most popular was called dichlorodiphenyl trichloroethan, also known as DDT.

144
00:10:12,660 --> 00:10:17,080
It hit the market with a real blast.

145
00:10:17,540 --> 00:10:21,440
It was effective against virtually every insect pest problem.

146
00:10:21,441 --> 00:10:24,760
It protected people from all kinds of diseases.

147
00:10:25,100 --> 00:10:28,580
So it was considered a miracle type of pesticide.

148
00:10:29,140 --> 00:10:31,760
But the euphoria lasted only a few years.

149
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:37,360
Well, DDT was the type of compound that had a long residual activity.

150
00:10:37,361 --> 00:10:43,120
And so as a result, certain pest populations became resistant to it very quickly.

151
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:48,060
Researchers found that some species of birds were unable to reproduce

152
00:10:48,061 --> 00:10:51,540
because residual DDT weakened the shells of their eggs.

153
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:56,000
And when the eggs broke prematurely, their offspring died.

154
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,320
Public concern arose about where DDT might turn up next,

155
00:11:00,960 --> 00:11:02,960
perhaps in the tissues of humans.

156
00:11:02,961 --> 00:11:12,020
In 1962, biologist Rachel Carson published a book titled Silent Spring.

157
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:14,660
It exposed the public hazards of DDT.

158
00:11:15,860 --> 00:11:18,380
At the time that Rachel Carson's book came out,

159
00:11:18,760 --> 00:11:22,860
it really mobilized those who knew more about pesticides

160
00:11:22,861 --> 00:11:27,980
and their toxicology and their effect on the environment.

161
00:11:27,981 --> 00:11:30,740
That really mobilized that community,

162
00:11:30,741 --> 00:11:33,640
and it started a revolution, if you will.

163
00:11:36,580 --> 00:11:42,020
In 1973, health officials banned the use of DDT in the United States.

164
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:46,140
But with malaria and West Nile virus still active threats,

165
00:11:47,560 --> 00:11:51,040
authorities know that the battle against the mosquito is far from over.

166
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:57,140
DDT remains in use in tropical areas in Asia and Africa.

167
00:11:57,141 --> 00:12:05,900
In those mosquito-infested zones, more than a million people die each year of malaria.

168
00:12:08,020 --> 00:12:12,680
About 2,500 different species of mosquito have been identified worldwide,

169
00:12:13,320 --> 00:12:16,420
with about 200 species found in the United States.

170
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:22,800
It seems to be the poster child for evil, the cockroach.

171
00:12:22,801 --> 00:12:27,200
Time on Earth, 340 million years.

172
00:12:27,940 --> 00:12:31,100
Characteristics, adaptable and nearly indestructible.

173
00:12:31,740 --> 00:12:34,480
Cockroaches have been around for hundreds of millions of years,

174
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:38,220
and certainly early men had to put up with cockroaches ever since they evolved.

175
00:12:39,020 --> 00:12:42,740
Whether it's on the ship's log of Captain Bly and his crew,

176
00:12:42,741 --> 00:12:48,460
or even back to caveman days, there were a lot of illustrations or drawings

177
00:12:48,461 --> 00:12:51,280
or engravings on the walls of caves

178
00:12:51,281 --> 00:12:53,960
that indicated that they had had to deal

179
00:12:53,961 --> 00:12:56,840
with the cockroach as a pest problem.

180
00:12:57,420 --> 00:13:00,020
Adult roaches can cram themselves into spaces

181
00:13:00,021 --> 00:13:02,380
as small as 3 16ths of an inch.

182
00:13:03,500 --> 00:13:04,980
A nymph or immature roach,

183
00:13:05,300 --> 00:13:07,740
as small as 1 52nd of an inch.

184
00:13:09,180 --> 00:13:10,540
On the cockroach menu,

185
00:13:10,860 --> 00:13:12,600
everything is the special of the day.

186
00:13:13,420 --> 00:13:15,580
Their gastronomic delights include garbage,

187
00:13:15,581 --> 00:13:18,280
used diapers, and even each other.

188
00:13:18,460 --> 00:13:22,580
Cannibalism occurs often when weaker roaches are picked off by stronger,

189
00:13:22,940 --> 00:13:24,780
or when two roaches are getting romantic,

190
00:13:25,140 --> 00:13:28,000
and a third decides they would make a good snack.

191
00:13:28,440 --> 00:13:30,340
They cause problems for humans as well

192
00:13:30,341 --> 00:13:33,300
by defecating in the places where we store food,

193
00:13:33,920 --> 00:13:36,240
and their feces and cast-off body parts

194
00:13:36,241 --> 00:13:39,200
have aggravated respiratory disease in children.

195
00:13:39,900 --> 00:13:42,960
You do see people reacting to cockroaches

196
00:13:42,961 --> 00:13:46,300
and to the feces associated with cockroaches,

197
00:13:46,301 --> 00:13:53,040
But when it comes to asthma and when it comes to allergies, yes, cockroaches are definitely a problem.

198
00:13:56,320 --> 00:14:00,420
Because of its warm climate and densely populated housing developments,

199
00:14:01,140 --> 00:14:06,200
Los Angeles has the dubious distinction of having more cockroaches than any other city in America.

200
00:14:07,500 --> 00:14:12,100
Exterminator Frederick Lee Reese has been battling cockroaches for 21 years.

201
00:14:12,340 --> 00:14:15,060
You could say it's the hunter's mentality.

202
00:14:15,061 --> 00:14:19,540
You're looking, you're seeking, you're trying to find the enemy, you might say, and destroy him.

203
00:14:20,360 --> 00:14:23,320
A common battlefield in this conflict can be a restaurant,

204
00:14:23,560 --> 00:14:28,900
where food, moisture, and abundant cracks and crevices make for a cockroach paradise.

205
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:33,020
Working at night when the business is closed,

206
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:38,000
these pesticide ninjas are about to turn a kitchen into a killing field.

207
00:14:38,460 --> 00:14:40,900
The first step is a top-to-bottom inspection.

208
00:14:40,901 --> 00:14:45,400
Well, in a visual inspection here, what I want to do is just actually find evidence.

209
00:14:45,401 --> 00:14:50,400
I want to find the actual roach itself, so I'm going to look at all my cracks and crevices,

210
00:14:50,401 --> 00:14:52,400
and a stove in particular.

211
00:14:52,401 --> 00:14:53,400
They love to stay in here.

212
00:14:53,401 --> 00:14:55,300
They can withstand a lot of heat.

213
00:14:55,301 --> 00:14:57,900
This is a refrigerator, and this is the molding.

214
00:14:57,901 --> 00:15:05,840
You can see the egg case right there, and then you can see the live roaches.

215
00:15:05,841 --> 00:15:10,380
The treatment consists of spraying a class of insecticides called pyrethrins into spaces

216
00:15:10,381 --> 00:15:14,666
in the walls. This will flush out the roaches.

217
00:15:14,667 --> 00:15:18,580
To distribute the pesticide into the wall,

218
00:15:19,340 --> 00:15:23,485
Reese's partner Jeffrey Campbell drills a series of holes.

219
00:15:23,486 --> 00:15:26,200
Compressed air from a portable sprayer

220
00:15:26,201 --> 00:15:30,720
forces the poison into the roaches' hiding places, bringing them out into the open.

221
00:15:31,720 --> 00:15:32,974
They do make a struggle.

222
00:15:32,975 --> 00:15:36,160
They'll come out, they'll turn in circles, move a little bit,

223
00:15:36,161 --> 00:15:39,800
and just turn upside down and die, depending on the resistance factor.

224
00:15:40,700 --> 00:15:44,000
Pyrethrins irritate the lining of the cockroach's respiratory tubes,

225
00:15:44,340 --> 00:15:46,900
but the effects are short-lived.

226
00:15:48,040 --> 00:15:51,600
That's why exterminators apply another, more commonplace substance,

227
00:15:52,340 --> 00:15:56,340
boric acid, to make the area even more inhospitable to the roaches.

228
00:15:57,380 --> 00:16:01,340
The boric acid is a stomach poison, and it gets on their exoskeleton,

229
00:16:01,460 --> 00:16:03,960
it will actually dehydrate it, or breaks through their exoskeleton,

230
00:16:03,961 --> 00:16:06,080
and if they do ingest it, it is a stomach poison.

231
00:16:06,160 --> 00:16:07,254
kills them too.

232
00:16:07,255 --> 00:16:12,580
A device called a duster electrostatically charges the boric acid powder.

233
00:16:13,180 --> 00:16:17,700
The charged powder sticks to the surfaces and remains in the path of roaches for weeks.

234
00:16:18,420 --> 00:16:21,960
While exterminators stay up nights thinking about new ways to kill a roach,

235
00:16:22,880 --> 00:16:26,240
entomologists spend their days studying its redeeming qualities.

236
00:16:26,900 --> 00:16:29,627
This is the Madagascar hissing cockroach.

237
00:16:29,628 --> 00:16:32,820
It's not native to this country, but is found in

238
00:16:32,821 --> 00:16:39,660
tropical areas of the world in the forest floor and converts cellulose material and plant material

239
00:16:39,661 --> 00:16:40,866
back into soil.

240
00:16:40,867 --> 00:16:47,780
It's become sort of a pet in that it's large, it's easy to handle, and then it hisses.

241
00:16:47,900 --> 00:16:53,900
When it's disturbed, it's a defensive mechanism that enables the cockroach to escape a predator

242
00:16:53,901 --> 00:16:59,463
by expelling air through its breathing pores that makes a hissing sound.

243
00:16:59,464 --> 00:17:01,240
But while some bugs may

244
00:17:01,241 --> 00:17:06,962
have admirers, most humans throughout history have wanted to get rid of them.

245
00:17:06,963 --> 00:17:08,300
The first recorded

246
00:17:08,301 --> 00:17:15,060
application of insecticides was in 2500 BC, when the Sumerians used sulfur compounds to control

247
00:17:15,061 --> 00:17:16,277
crop-eating insects.

248
00:17:16,278 --> 00:17:20,900
And pyrethrins, used even today to kill roaches in a Los Angeles restaurant,

249
00:17:20,901 --> 00:17:23,367
have been around for centuries.

250
00:17:23,368 --> 00:17:28,380
It was in the 17th or 18th century that people realized that by

251
00:17:28,381 --> 00:17:31,080
using these ground-up chrysanthemum flowers

252
00:17:31,081 --> 00:17:35,500
that they could keep insects out of their living area

253
00:17:35,501 --> 00:17:37,320
or protect their food supplies.

254
00:17:37,980 --> 00:17:41,720
So it has been several centuries that pyrethrum

255
00:17:41,721 --> 00:17:44,080
and the ground-up chrysanthemum flower

256
00:17:44,081 --> 00:17:46,720
has been used as an insecticide.

257
00:17:47,320 --> 00:17:51,500
By 1800, French grape growers were using hydrated lime,

258
00:17:51,840 --> 00:17:54,840
known as Paris green, and also copper compounds

259
00:17:54,841 --> 00:17:57,560
to protect grapes against insect damage

260
00:17:57,561 --> 00:17:59,240
and human poachers.

261
00:17:59,760 --> 00:18:02,000
The foul-tasting substances did the trick.

262
00:18:02,960 --> 00:18:06,380
But at the same time, when it came time to take the grapes

263
00:18:06,381 --> 00:18:10,300
and produce wine or other products from grapes,

264
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:14,620
they were perfectly okay because they could be cleaned prior to production.

265
00:18:15,420 --> 00:18:17,700
In 1880, the next step came,

266
00:18:18,020 --> 00:18:20,560
when the first commercial sprayers allowed exterminators

267
00:18:20,561 --> 00:18:23,540
to deliver pesticides in a mist of fine droplets.

268
00:18:23,541 --> 00:18:32,540
Older formulations like Paris Green fell into disfavor to be replaced by cheaper and more effective chemicals, like lead arsenate.

269
00:18:33,540 --> 00:18:39,540
When larger areas were to be treated, agricultural scientists dreamed of dispersing pesticides from the air.

270
00:18:40,540 --> 00:18:45,540
In 1921, the battle against the Sphinx moth went airborne over Ohio.

271
00:18:46,540 --> 00:18:50,540
The pest was defoliating an ornamental species of tree called the Catalpa.

272
00:18:50,541 --> 00:18:51,241
A U.S.

273
00:18:51,242 --> 00:18:59,240
Army pilot flew a modified Curtis JN-6 Super Jenny and dropped lead arsenate dust on the infestation.

274
00:19:00,080 --> 00:19:03,720
It was the first aerial application of an insecticide.

275
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:10,860
And suddenly, chemical companies that made agricultural pesticides saw the future.

276
00:19:11,620 --> 00:19:14,460
Fields could be sprayed quickly and efficiently from the air.

277
00:19:14,461 --> 00:19:24,460
During World War II, German manufacturers like IG Farben and American firms like DuPont

278
00:19:24,461 --> 00:19:29,460
developed organophosphates for use in chemical warfare.

279
00:19:29,461 --> 00:19:35,460
Later in peacetime, the same chemicals were adapted for use as potent pesticides.

280
00:19:35,461 --> 00:19:40,460
But chemical companies envisioned a swath of destruction and a stream of revenue

281
00:19:40,461 --> 00:19:43,660
that extended beyond croplands or the battlefield.

282
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:49,860
And so as a result, in the structural markets for structural pest control,

283
00:19:50,240 --> 00:19:53,460
we were also looking for ways to eradicate pests.

284
00:19:53,461 --> 00:19:58,560
You were actually able to take those formulations and do better pest control for homeowners,

285
00:19:58,880 --> 00:20:01,360
and it was a good way for them to expand their markets as well.

286
00:20:05,680 --> 00:20:08,980
Humication became a favored way to dispatch pests in the home.

287
00:20:08,981 --> 00:20:17,220
Bug bombs and aerosol sprays, based on organophosphates or pyrethroids, lined the shelves of hardware stores.

288
00:20:17,221 --> 00:20:22,100
These sprays and fumigants work with all the delicacy of a sledgehammer,

289
00:20:22,101 --> 00:20:34,900
putting the herd on anything that crawled, flew, or walked, including roaches, ants, flies, crickets, cats, dogs, and small children.

290
00:20:34,901 --> 00:20:43,380
In the 1960s, after the wake-up call to the residual properties of DDT, the industry started

291
00:20:43,381 --> 00:20:49,340
to research alternatives to poisons and began to develop more sophisticated controls, such

292
00:20:49,341 --> 00:20:50,500
as pheromonal lures.

293
00:20:51,540 --> 00:20:56,880
These are traps baited with a synthetic sexual attractant that lures a cockroach into a glue

294
00:20:56,881 --> 00:20:59,120
trap, where it becomes stuck.

295
00:21:00,120 --> 00:21:02,720
Other chemicals interfere with the insect growth cycle.

296
00:21:02,721 --> 00:21:09,560
So we use what's called juvenile hormone analogs, whether it's for mosquitoes or whether it's for cockroaches.

297
00:21:09,561 --> 00:21:16,320
It keeps the insect in the juvenile stage and prevents it from becoming a reproductive adult.

298
00:21:16,740 --> 00:21:23,340
So in effect, it is a sterilant and keeps the insect from being able to reproduce itself.

299
00:21:23,940 --> 00:21:28,460
Clearly, cockroach control has benefited from such advances in chemical formulations.

300
00:21:28,461 --> 00:21:33,020
But cockroaches can reproduce faster than the exterminator can kill them.

301
00:21:35,100 --> 00:21:43,340
Theoretically, in a year's time, a single mating pair of cockroaches can multiply to a population of 350,000.

302
00:21:44,680 --> 00:21:50,360
Each year, the pest control industry in America averages more than $5 billion in sales.

303
00:21:52,840 --> 00:21:54,400
Enter the rat.

304
00:21:54,401 --> 00:22:01,480
carrier of the Black Plague, a.k.a. the devil's lapdog.

305
00:22:02,200 --> 00:22:05,200
Rats can chew through glass, metal, and concrete

306
00:22:05,201 --> 00:22:08,840
and can fit inside a pipe as small as half an inch.

307
00:22:10,080 --> 00:22:13,380
Rats destroy an estimated 20% of the world's food crop

308
00:22:13,381 --> 00:22:14,980
before it ever gets to market.

309
00:22:16,260 --> 00:22:19,000
In some areas, you would never think to find them.

310
00:22:19,001 --> 00:22:21,420
For instance, in Arizona, there was just an issue

311
00:22:21,421 --> 00:22:25,700
where there was a town of homes that were over half a million dollars,

312
00:22:25,920 --> 00:22:29,180
and they had a massive infestation of roof rats.

313
00:22:29,920 --> 00:22:32,900
Another haven for rats is New York City.

314
00:22:33,520 --> 00:22:36,960
The wily rodents infest buildings near subways and parks,

315
00:22:37,440 --> 00:22:40,960
where they seek food and easy access to escape tunnels.

316
00:22:42,420 --> 00:22:45,940
James Gibson is an assistant commissioner for the New York Department of Health.

317
00:22:45,941 --> 00:22:52,940
He oversees a $13 million budget allocated to exterminating rodents in problem properties

318
00:22:52,941 --> 00:22:54,940
and responding to citizen complaints.

319
00:22:54,941 --> 00:22:58,940
We get letters from elected officials, we get letters from members of the public,

320
00:22:58,941 --> 00:23:01,940
we get phone calls from the public directly to our offices.

321
00:23:01,941 --> 00:23:03,940
We handle all of those complaints.

322
00:23:03,941 --> 00:23:08,940
He's on his way to meet a team of battle-hardened soldiers in the city's war against rodents.

323
00:23:08,941 --> 00:23:10,940
Yeah, nice seeing you too.

324
00:23:10,941 --> 00:23:16,980
He supervises a total of 35 inspectors and 30 exterminators.

325
00:23:17,700 --> 00:23:20,020
On a good day, they kill hundreds of rats,

326
00:23:20,260 --> 00:23:22,340
and they know what they're up against.

327
00:23:24,260 --> 00:23:25,620
Picture the size of a cat.

328
00:23:26,620 --> 00:23:30,720
The size of a cat and sometimes the fattest cat that you can see.

329
00:23:31,460 --> 00:23:33,220
That's the size a rat can be.

330
00:23:33,940 --> 00:23:37,180
Gibson's inspectors brief him on the condition of the infested building.

331
00:23:37,820 --> 00:23:42,640
They take note of nearby food sources, such as garbage, in and near the building,

332
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,440
and are aware of the rats' favorite escape routes.

333
00:23:46,160 --> 00:23:51,460
So basically what they're doing is they're burrowing in here and weakening the infrastructure of this brick wall.

334
00:23:51,461 --> 00:23:58,060
They actually can gnaw through concrete, and they'll have nests all throughout here going into the soil on this other side of the wall.

335
00:23:59,100 --> 00:24:01,900
Time to send in the city's rat commandos.

336
00:24:02,280 --> 00:24:04,900
exterminator Larry Adams journeys into the basement

337
00:24:04,901 --> 00:24:08,420
to check out one of the worst infestations he's ever seen.

338
00:24:08,860 --> 00:24:12,760
This is a situation where I would tell a landowner, a homeowner,

339
00:24:12,840 --> 00:24:16,480
do not, under any circumstance, put a cat down in this basement.

340
00:24:17,320 --> 00:24:19,600
Because I've had people argue with me, oh, cats are for rats.

341
00:24:19,740 --> 00:24:20,620
No, cats are for mice.

342
00:24:21,620 --> 00:24:22,560
Rats eat cats.

343
00:24:23,620 --> 00:24:24,320
Okay?

344
00:24:24,500 --> 00:24:26,500
No, any cat with any sense is not coming down here.

345
00:24:26,700 --> 00:24:29,020
And with nearly 30 years' experience,

346
00:24:29,021 --> 00:24:32,540
he's seen apartments swarming with unwelcome furry visitors.

347
00:24:33,320 --> 00:24:36,100
The good engine is they create the burrows, they dig holes.

348
00:24:36,420 --> 00:24:38,940
If it rains, they dig in such a way that they won't drown.

349
00:24:39,480 --> 00:24:43,020
They'll dig a hole down the head, then they dig another one a little further up

350
00:24:43,021 --> 00:24:44,940
and go, that's a rainwater that stops here.

351
00:24:45,160 --> 00:24:45,860
They're very smart.

352
00:24:47,280 --> 00:24:50,680
Rats are among the smartest pests an exterminator might encounter.

353
00:24:52,300 --> 00:24:56,100
They won't feed on bait if they have seen other rats die after eating it.

354
00:24:56,101 --> 00:25:00,100
Accordingly, exterminators will use slow-acting poisons,

355
00:25:00,101 --> 00:25:03,100
called multi-dose poisons by the trade.

356
00:25:03,101 --> 00:25:06,100
The rats feed on the packaged bait for weeks,

357
00:25:06,101 --> 00:25:09,100
never suspecting that their meal is laced with toxins.

358
00:25:09,101 --> 00:25:12,100
We use single-dose and multi-dose anticoagulant bait.

359
00:25:12,101 --> 00:25:14,100
Let me explain what an anticoagulant is.

360
00:25:14,101 --> 00:25:21,100
Anticoagulant inhibits the body's ability or blood's ability to coagulate, to gel, you see?

361
00:25:21,101 --> 00:25:27,300
Therefore, what happens is the animal actually drowns its own body fluid, to put it simply.

362
00:25:28,340 --> 00:25:30,320
Aside from their use as a rat poison,

363
00:25:31,040 --> 00:25:35,160
anticoagulants are also used in low dosages as a human heart medication.

364
00:25:36,980 --> 00:25:42,700
Warfarin is one example of an anticoagulant drug used to prevent blood clots that might damage vital organs.

365
00:25:43,680 --> 00:25:46,040
One species' blessing is another's bane.

366
00:25:46,640 --> 00:25:50,560
Rat exterminators will follow rat tracks or trails of rat droppings.

367
00:25:51,100 --> 00:25:53,360
to find the highest concentration of rats.

368
00:25:53,600 --> 00:25:54,980
All of that is rat drop.

369
00:25:55,240 --> 00:25:56,320
There's thousands of them.

370
00:25:56,680 --> 00:25:58,100
And place their poisons there.

371
00:25:58,820 --> 00:26:01,660
The exterminator will use different kinds of rat bait

372
00:26:01,661 --> 00:26:03,800
in case the rats are fussy eaters.

373
00:26:04,940 --> 00:26:06,240
All the poisons are good.

374
00:26:06,320 --> 00:26:07,040
All of them are killed.

375
00:26:07,480 --> 00:26:08,620
That's what they're designed for.

376
00:26:08,980 --> 00:26:11,260
But if it doesn't have a good attractant in it,

377
00:26:12,160 --> 00:26:12,880
they're not going to eat it.

378
00:26:13,040 --> 00:26:15,380
Not when they have rice and beef, pork chop bones,

379
00:26:15,960 --> 00:26:17,360
understand spaghetti and meatballs.

380
00:26:18,200 --> 00:26:19,440
If you were a rat, you had a choice

381
00:26:19,441 --> 00:26:23,640
between spaghetti and meatballs, lasagna, and this, okay?

382
00:26:24,660 --> 00:26:28,120
Adams expects to see results, meaning lots of dead rats,

383
00:26:28,500 --> 00:26:30,700
in the basement within four or five days.

384
00:26:31,060 --> 00:26:33,240
But that doesn't mean his job here is done.

385
00:26:34,240 --> 00:26:36,440
Then we'll come back and retreat it.

386
00:26:36,940 --> 00:26:38,480
And we'll keep coming back and retreating it

387
00:26:38,481 --> 00:26:40,560
until the population's diminished, you know,

388
00:26:40,561 --> 00:26:43,120
to a point where they don't present a problem anymore.

389
00:26:43,980 --> 00:26:46,280
Larry Adams is too much of a gentleman to brag

390
00:26:46,281 --> 00:26:48,320
that he's the best rat killer in the city,

391
00:26:48,321 --> 00:26:50,080
or claim that he's seen it all.

392
00:26:51,160 --> 00:26:52,800
Look, the rats have a pool table.

393
00:26:55,220 --> 00:26:56,200
Sports enthusiasts.

394
00:26:59,380 --> 00:27:01,040
But he does have a good reason

395
00:27:01,041 --> 00:27:03,200
for pursuing New York's rats to the death.

396
00:27:03,840 --> 00:27:05,580
I came up in a poor neighborhood,

397
00:27:06,020 --> 00:27:08,300
and I've seen what rats can do to people.

398
00:27:08,440 --> 00:27:10,500
I've seen rat babies with their lips

399
00:27:10,501 --> 00:27:13,060
have been bitten by rats, fingertips chewed by rats.

400
00:27:13,061 --> 00:27:15,080
So it's kind of gratifying being able to help

401
00:27:15,081 --> 00:27:17,160
the poorer part of society get rid of the rats

402
00:27:17,161 --> 00:27:20,400
because rats, they're really not particular about where they'd be

403
00:27:20,401 --> 00:27:22,180
as long as they have the stuff they need to live.

404
00:27:23,440 --> 00:27:29,000
A pair of rats and their offspring can produce 1,500 more rats in only one year,

405
00:27:29,340 --> 00:27:30,480
if they all survived.

406
00:27:31,540 --> 00:27:36,100
But through history, the math concerning the rat population has always been daunting.

407
00:27:37,180 --> 00:27:43,660
As early as 13 B.C., the Romans successfully stopped rats from infesting grain storage areas.

408
00:27:43,661 --> 00:27:54,760
They raised granaries atop stone slabs to make it harder for the rats to gain entry.

409
00:27:54,761 --> 00:27:59,020
Rats contributed to mass death during the bubonic plague of the Middle Ages, called

410
00:27:59,021 --> 00:28:01,660
the Black Death.

411
00:28:01,661 --> 00:28:05,320
The plague was incredibly devastating in certain European countries.

412
00:28:05,321 --> 00:28:09,780
We're talking about 80, 85% of entire cities wiped out, you know, hundreds of thousands

413
00:28:09,781 --> 00:28:11,820
of people.

414
00:28:11,821 --> 00:28:17,000
The plague vector was not the rat itself, but an infected flea joyriding on the rat's back.

415
00:28:18,220 --> 00:28:21,640
Early rat catchers relied more on superstition than science.

416
00:28:22,120 --> 00:28:28,120
It was believed that a rat that had been brushed with tar and oil and released would scare off the rest of the pack,

417
00:28:28,600 --> 00:28:34,320
and ship captains thought that setting one rat afire aboard a ship would cause the rest to jump overboard.

418
00:28:34,900 --> 00:28:37,020
The rats were smarter than that.

419
00:28:37,021 --> 00:28:45,020
Rats probably got to the east coast of our country in the early 1600s on ships from European settlers.

420
00:28:46,020 --> 00:28:49,520
Until that time, rats were unknown on this continent.

421
00:28:50,580 --> 00:28:53,480
Eventually, shipboard rats brought the plague to America.

422
00:28:54,680 --> 00:29:00,080
In the continental United States, the first plague case was noted on March 6, 1900.

423
00:29:00,960 --> 00:29:03,980
The battle against the rat continued in the new century.

424
00:29:03,981 --> 00:29:10,160
dogs were enlisted as ratters who dragged the menace from its lair and broke its neck.

425
00:29:11,160 --> 00:29:15,100
Some of the earlier chemicals that were used to control rats were arsenic,

426
00:29:15,300 --> 00:29:20,580
paste, they mixed it in with the food, sodium fluoride powder that was mixed in with the food,

427
00:29:21,220 --> 00:29:25,620
and calcium cyanide, which was a powder that released cyanide gas.

428
00:29:27,240 --> 00:29:31,260
Metal traps, adapted from larger animal traps already in use,

429
00:29:31,520 --> 00:29:33,560
also clamped down on the rat problem.

430
00:29:33,980 --> 00:29:40,560
While rats are better known as carriers of disease, their smaller cousins, mice, have

431
00:29:40,561 --> 00:29:42,640
also spread misery.

432
00:29:42,641 --> 00:29:47,660
Hantavirus, a deadly lung infection, is carried by field mice.

433
00:29:47,661 --> 00:29:52,280
At least 300 Americans have become ill after inadvertently coming in contact with mouse

434
00:29:52,281 --> 00:29:54,960
urine and droppings.

435
00:29:54,961 --> 00:29:58,620
More than 120 have died.

436
00:29:58,621 --> 00:30:01,800
For centuries, cats were the mouse killers of choice.

437
00:30:01,801 --> 00:30:06,380
But some cats were automated out of their jobs, when the familiar wood and wire mouse

438
00:30:06,381 --> 00:30:11,680
trap was patented in 1899 by John Mast of Pennsylvania.

439
00:30:11,681 --> 00:30:16,500
His design, basically unchanged, is still manufactured in Pennsylvania by the Woodstream

440
00:30:16,501 --> 00:30:17,500
Corporation.

441
00:30:17,501 --> 00:30:23,340
It also is probably one of the most cost efficient ways for a consumer to deal with

442
00:30:23,341 --> 00:30:24,660
a mouse problem.

443
00:30:24,661 --> 00:30:31,240
We sell tens of millions of these traps a year, which speaks to their popularity and

444
00:30:31,241 --> 00:30:32,599
their effectiveness.

445
00:30:32,600 --> 00:30:37,900
The classic mousetrap has a stiff piece of wire called a bale that is attached

446
00:30:37,901 --> 00:30:38,683
to a trigger.

447
00:30:38,684 --> 00:30:43,800
A bit of cheese, or more popularly peanut butter, is placed on the trigger to attract

448
00:30:43,801 --> 00:30:44,840
a hungry mouse.

449
00:30:44,841 --> 00:30:50,180
Once tripped by the mouse, a spring-loaded wire snaps shut at the speed of 65

450
00:30:50,181 --> 00:30:56,900
miles per hour. It's a simple and much imitated method of murder.

451
00:31:01,240 --> 00:31:07,580
Today, manufacturers are marketing mouse traps that can be set with one hand.

452
00:31:07,581 --> 00:31:11,960
Traps that tastefully hide the catch inside a cardboard box.

453
00:31:11,961 --> 00:31:20,200
Extra strength traps, strong enough to put the hammer on the nastiest of rats.

454
00:31:20,201 --> 00:31:24,100
And even a trap that spares the captive's life.

455
00:31:24,101 --> 00:31:30,180
There are some people who would prefer to catch and release.

456
00:31:30,181 --> 00:31:34,240
So we make traps for people who have that preference.

457
00:31:34,241 --> 00:31:38,660
With the wide variety of mouse traps already in the marketplace, you'd think modern inventors

458
00:31:38,661 --> 00:31:41,120
would look for something else to invent.

459
00:31:41,121 --> 00:31:43,560
Not so, says Woodstream's president.

460
00:31:43,561 --> 00:31:48,800
Sometimes his phone rings off the hook with ideas for a better way to dispatch mice.

461
00:31:48,801 --> 00:31:52,060
It's not just calls, it's letters, emails more and more.

462
00:31:52,061 --> 00:31:53,500
And we listen.

463
00:31:53,501 --> 00:31:54,820
There's an old saying.

464
00:31:54,821 --> 00:31:58,120
Some people attribute it to Ralph Waldo Emerson.

465
00:31:58,121 --> 00:32:01,060
I have not been able to establish that he in fact said this,

466
00:32:01,160 --> 00:32:03,160
so maybe in your research you can,

467
00:32:03,300 --> 00:32:08,640
but build the better mousetrap and the world will beat a path to your door.

468
00:32:09,240 --> 00:32:11,900
I think many take that quite literally.

469
00:32:12,340 --> 00:32:16,400
For some, glue traps represent an improvement in mousetrap design.

470
00:32:17,200 --> 00:32:18,900
Laced with a bait to attract rodents,

471
00:32:19,440 --> 00:32:22,460
they get their deadly and sticky job done silently,

472
00:32:22,461 --> 00:32:25,560
but the hapless victim is often alive and struggling.

473
00:32:25,561 --> 00:32:27,560
You can drown him in the toilet.

474
00:32:27,561 --> 00:32:29,560
It takes but a minute.

475
00:32:29,561 --> 00:32:32,560
Crush his little skull with a hammer.

476
00:32:32,561 --> 00:32:37,560
Or pop him in the freezer for a slow chill to the beyond.

477
00:32:37,561 --> 00:32:43,560
But if you don't want a mouse among your leftovers, take heart.

478
00:32:43,561 --> 00:32:45,560
There are still more ways to kill them.

479
00:32:45,561 --> 00:32:50,560
In fact, the US Patent Office lists more than 4,000 mousetrap designs.

480
00:32:50,561 --> 00:32:59,040
designs. The average female rat has sex 22 times a day.

481
00:32:59,041 --> 00:33:04,140
Living in colonies numbering 60,000 or more individuals, with an elaborate social order

482
00:33:04,141 --> 00:33:09,380
to which they adhere with military precision, termites are one of the most destructive insects

483
00:33:09,381 --> 00:33:10,380
in the world.

484
00:33:10,381 --> 00:33:16,460
Termites in the U.S. every year do over $2 billion worth of damage.

485
00:33:16,461 --> 00:33:21,941
The termite. Special abilities? Able to work undercover.

486
00:33:21,942 --> 00:33:24,780
Insatiable appetite for wood.

487
00:33:26,220 --> 00:33:29,360
Termites can mount an attack on structures from several fronts.

488
00:33:30,440 --> 00:33:33,591
The subterranean variety prefers dark, damp places.

489
00:33:33,592 --> 00:33:36,000
They do the most damage in the shortest

490
00:33:36,001 --> 00:33:40,680
amount of time. Dry wood termites turn up anywhere there's a food source.

491
00:33:40,681 --> 00:33:44,820
Some experts predict that under ideal conditions,

492
00:33:45,520 --> 00:33:51,020
a termite colony of 60,000 workers is capable of consuming a one-foot length of 2x4

493
00:33:51,021 --> 00:33:53,280
in about five months.

494
00:33:54,440 --> 00:33:59,900
This is a piece of structural timber that has been infested and invaded by termites.

495
00:34:00,840 --> 00:34:06,300
And as you can see, the termites eat the softwood layers of the wood tissue,

496
00:34:06,301 --> 00:34:10,100
and then the pieces that you see left here are hardwood layers.

497
00:34:10,680 --> 00:34:16,420
But while we think of termites as a pest, they certainly have their place in the world's ecology.

498
00:34:17,420 --> 00:34:22,020
Termites fit into the ecosystem like a lot of other insects do.

499
00:34:22,440 --> 00:34:23,980
Their job is to recycle.

500
00:34:24,440 --> 00:34:27,000
Termites break down wood and cellulose.

501
00:34:27,220 --> 00:34:31,660
This is a very important part of the living part of the earth as it occurs.

502
00:34:33,780 --> 00:34:36,780
In warm weather states like California and Florida,

503
00:34:36,781 --> 00:34:40,980
homes are often pre-treated to kill termites even before the human owners

504
00:34:40,981 --> 00:34:46,560
move in. This takes the form of laying down a liquid barrier of poison around

505
00:34:46,561 --> 00:34:52,080
the foundation or by erecting a tent and fumigating the entire structure. But when

506
00:34:52,081 --> 00:34:56,520
an infestation is large, spreading poison everywhere isn't considered safe or

507
00:34:56,521 --> 00:35:01,140
practical. Exterminators have developed methods that take advantage of termite

508
00:35:01,141 --> 00:35:05,900
behavior in order to kill them more efficiently.

509
00:35:05,901 --> 00:35:12,860
Welcome to the largest termite baiting project in the world, 92 acres of termite territory.

510
00:35:12,861 --> 00:35:19,900
On this condo development in Southern California, 115 out of 129 structures are infested with

511
00:35:19,901 --> 00:35:22,200
termites.

512
00:35:22,201 --> 00:35:26,060
Exterminators are taking action by installing a system that works something like a burglar

513
00:35:26,061 --> 00:35:31,060
alarm to alert humans to insect invaders.

514
00:35:31,061 --> 00:35:38,600
At the hub of the system is a series of bait stations that the exterminator puts into the ground at regular intervals around an infested structure.

515
00:35:39,480 --> 00:35:43,400
To date, we have over 8,000 bait stations on this site.

516
00:35:44,620 --> 00:35:48,300
And we've utilized over 600 of the bait tube devices.

517
00:35:50,880 --> 00:35:59,580
After the bait stations are in place for about a month, the exterminator uses a device called an interrogator to check for termite activity.

518
00:36:01,060 --> 00:36:08,060
An audible tone alerts him to the location of each bait station.

519
00:36:08,061 --> 00:36:12,060
Inside each bait station is something the termites like to eat.

520
00:36:12,061 --> 00:36:16,400
This is only wood and it is for monitoring to intercept the termites.

521
00:36:16,401 --> 00:36:18,380
There is no active ingredient.

522
00:36:18,381 --> 00:36:22,380
It's simply a resource for the termites to feed on.

523
00:36:22,381 --> 00:36:28,340
But between those pieces of wood is a thin strip of electrically conductive material.

524
00:36:28,341 --> 00:36:36,300
When the termites chew the wood, they break an electrical circuit, setting off an alarm.

525
00:36:36,301 --> 00:36:39,680
That signals the exterminator to check the bait station for activity.

526
00:36:39,681 --> 00:36:43,740
Pretty good-sized colony of termites here.

527
00:36:43,741 --> 00:36:48,200
Then the exterminator replaces the wood with a termite bait that contains an insect growth

528
00:36:48,201 --> 00:36:49,200
regulator.

529
00:36:49,201 --> 00:36:54,000
We collect the termites, we then place them in the bait tube which contains the active

530
00:36:54,001 --> 00:36:54,701
ingredient.

531
00:36:54,702 --> 00:36:59,160
That's hexaflumarion, an insect growth regulator that prevents the natural

532
00:36:59,161 --> 00:37:03,440
molting process of the termite. When they can't successfully complete the molt, they

533
00:37:03,441 --> 00:37:06,920
die and you get a crash of the colony.

534
00:37:06,921 --> 00:37:12,300
The termites in the tube take the bait laced with insect growth regulator back to the colony

535
00:37:12,301 --> 00:37:15,974
and pass it on to their cellulose chewing comrades.

536
00:37:15,975 --> 00:37:19,000
Within 30 to 60 days, the entire colony is

537
00:37:19,001 --> 00:37:19,701
wiped out.

538
00:37:19,702 --> 00:37:24,160
Using data gathered from each bait station and downloaded onto a computer, the

539
00:37:24,161 --> 00:37:25,951
system continually tracks activity.

540
00:37:25,952 --> 00:37:28,560
If the termites were to come back, the exterminator

541
00:37:28,561 --> 00:37:34,400
simply rebates. For drywood termite jobs, there are alternatives.

542
00:37:35,540 --> 00:37:40,640
We're using clean, hot air. We're not using poisonous gases, and that's the benefit,

543
00:37:40,900 --> 00:37:44,031
and people nowadays are looking for alternative methods.

544
00:37:44,032 --> 00:37:45,820
The temperature has to be raised

545
00:37:45,821 --> 00:37:48,960
up to 130 degrees for more than 22 minutes.

546
00:37:49,360 --> 00:37:51,620
And once that's accomplished, then the termites are dead.

547
00:37:52,160 --> 00:37:57,660
Propane tanks fitted with blowers send hot air through ducting right to where the termites are,

548
00:37:58,000 --> 00:37:58,940
even up in an attic.

549
00:38:00,020 --> 00:38:05,360
Make no mistake, 130 degrees is hot, even for the exterminators on the job.

550
00:38:06,040 --> 00:38:06,740
Pretty dry.

551
00:38:06,741 --> 00:38:10,860
You got a lot of water, and you just got to keep yourself from being dehydrated.

552
00:38:10,861 --> 00:38:16,880
A system of thermocouple sensors which remotely monitor the heat output of the propane tanks

553
00:38:16,881 --> 00:38:21,620
keep the job tolerable for the homeowner, but intolerable for the termite.

554
00:38:23,580 --> 00:38:28,640
Another effective spot treatment involves cranking up the voltage with an electrogun

555
00:38:28,641 --> 00:38:34,020
capable of zapping 90,000 volts of electricity through the wall and into the termites.

556
00:38:34,460 --> 00:38:38,540
This is called the bazooka, one of two extensions to the electrogun that we have.

557
00:38:38,541 --> 00:38:41,940
We're going to zap this piece of wood right here.

558
00:38:41,941 --> 00:38:44,380
It's a wood member at door jam.

559
00:38:44,381 --> 00:38:46,900
What we do is scan for the termites.

560
00:38:46,901 --> 00:38:50,980
Since they're made mostly of water and it's a better conductor than wood, if they're inside

561
00:38:50,981 --> 00:38:54,360
the wood, it's going to go straight to them and take them out.

562
00:38:54,361 --> 00:39:01,420
For the unfortunate termites in the path of the electro gun, it's a shocking death.

563
00:39:01,421 --> 00:39:06,120
While these alternative methods of extermination result in more precise kills and lower body

564
00:39:06,121 --> 00:39:10,320
counts than the traditional methods such as fumigation.

565
00:39:10,321 --> 00:39:13,680
There is no love lost between the hunter and the hunted.

566
00:39:13,681 --> 00:39:16,180
Termites, they're going to be around forever.

567
00:39:16,181 --> 00:39:17,920
You know, they have a place, and they

568
00:39:17,921 --> 00:39:20,060
can be out in the rainforest, and they can chew up the trees,

569
00:39:20,061 --> 00:39:21,220
but not in our homes.

570
00:39:24,060 --> 00:39:26,900
The only US state where termites have not been found

571
00:39:26,901 --> 00:39:32,960
is Alaska, where they're fought up in the clear blue sky

572
00:39:32,961 --> 00:39:36,100
or down in dank subterranean layers.

573
00:39:36,101 --> 00:39:38,920
The battle against insects, rodents, and other nuisances

574
00:39:38,921 --> 00:39:42,060
has pressed the so-called dominant life form on Earth

575
00:39:42,061 --> 00:39:46,080
to constantly up the ante, betting that advances in technology

576
00:39:46,081 --> 00:39:48,440
will help us stamp out pests.

577
00:39:48,441 --> 00:39:51,160
But the lessons of ecology, most notably

578
00:39:51,161 --> 00:39:53,520
the growing understanding of the interconnectedness

579
00:39:53,521 --> 00:39:57,120
of all things, have shaped the future of extermination

580
00:39:57,121 --> 00:40:00,400
toward a more holistic approach.

581
00:40:00,401 --> 00:40:04,520
the watchword with pests is now control, not total annihilation.

582
00:40:05,800 --> 00:40:09,480
Toward that end, Integrated Pest Management, or IPM,

583
00:40:09,920 --> 00:40:13,620
concentrates on eliminating three things pests need to survive.

584
00:40:14,760 --> 00:40:18,020
Eliminate the food source, eliminate the water source,

585
00:40:18,440 --> 00:40:20,900
and then treat for the population that's there.

586
00:40:20,901 --> 00:40:25,900
We no longer just go in and spray to eradicate a particular area

587
00:40:26,480 --> 00:40:27,760
which may or may not work.

588
00:40:27,761 --> 00:40:33,400
What we do is we put the material where the pests are, not where the people are.

589
00:40:33,800 --> 00:40:38,660
The environmentally sensitive approach concentrates on thorough inspections to find the pests

590
00:40:38,661 --> 00:40:42,060
and then mounts a surgical strike on small areas.

591
00:40:43,240 --> 00:40:47,840
The pest control industry is also enlisting other species to help us in our battle for control.

592
00:40:48,980 --> 00:40:50,540
For instance, there's a killfish.

593
00:40:50,820 --> 00:40:54,620
In the industry, we are using this as a biological control for mosquitoes.

594
00:40:54,621 --> 00:41:00,620
The fish eats mosquitoes and thrives in ponds where there are lots of larvae.

595
00:41:00,621 --> 00:41:04,620
Nematode worms may help to control termite colonies.

596
00:41:04,621 --> 00:41:11,620
They carry a toxin that slowly kills the wood-munching menaces, laying waste to the colony.

597
00:41:11,621 --> 00:41:18,620
Exterminators have searched for high-tech ways to kill mosquitoes by exploiting the way that insect tracks its prey.

598
00:41:18,621 --> 00:41:23,620
Mosquitoes sense streams of carbon dioxide gas, exhaled by all mammals.

599
00:41:24,620 --> 00:41:31,620
One device new to the market generates carbon dioxide from propane gas, attracting mosquitoes into a one-way trap.

600
00:41:31,621 --> 00:41:35,620
Mosquitoes fly in, but they don't fly out.

601
00:41:35,621 --> 00:41:43,620
And the mosquito is under fire from gadgets, like the insector, that lure the tiny buzzing beasts to their demise.

602
00:41:43,621 --> 00:41:47,020
But these killing machines are indiscriminate.

603
00:41:47,021 --> 00:41:49,420
Entomologists see that as a drawback.

604
00:41:49,421 --> 00:41:53,220
For people who have bug zappers and put them out in their backyard,

605
00:41:53,221 --> 00:41:56,620
they have to remember that those things not only kill bad bugs,

606
00:41:56,621 --> 00:41:58,620
but they kill good bugs as well.

607
00:41:58,621 --> 00:42:04,620
Century by century, the war continues against the mosquito,

608
00:42:04,621 --> 00:42:07,620
the rat, the termite, the cockroach,

609
00:42:07,621 --> 00:42:09,620
and all their creepy crawly brethren.

610
00:42:09,621 --> 00:42:11,620
We can win this war.

611
00:42:11,621 --> 00:42:15,120
win this war, we're going to need the participation of the residents, of businesses, of government

612
00:42:15,121 --> 00:42:19,523
agencies. Everyone needs to cooperate in this effort in order for us to win.

613
00:42:19,524 --> 00:42:20,224
Or else the

614
00:42:20,225 --> 00:42:20,925
rats will win.

615
00:42:23,100 --> 00:42:27,840
Though the weapons have become increasingly high-tech and the foot soldier, the exterminator,

616
00:42:28,080 --> 00:42:33,720
has become more sophisticated and ecologically aware, there is one advantage pests have over

617
00:42:33,721 --> 00:42:37,783
humans. They outnumber us by about 16 billion to one.

618
00:42:37,784 --> 00:42:41,080
At any time, there are about 10 quintillion

619
00:42:41,081 --> 00:42:47,440
insects alive. Our best hope, aside from the next best killer chemical, or by

620
00:42:47,441 --> 00:42:51,400
recruiting other species to help us, may be to seek a path toward peaceful

621
00:42:51,401 --> 00:42:57,280
coexistence. It's educating the consumer about what to do about them and then

622
00:42:57,281 --> 00:43:02,840
about how to live with them and manage them. I don't know whether we can ever

623
00:43:02,841 --> 00:43:08,680
control them to the point where we will eradicate them, nor am I convinced that

624
00:43:08,681 --> 00:43:10,440
that's the right thing to do.

625
00:43:10,441 --> 00:43:12,520
And if you were to have a world like that,

626
00:43:12,521 --> 00:43:16,480
you wouldn't have flowers that would be pollinated by insects.

627
00:43:16,481 --> 00:43:18,820
You wouldn't have honey that would be produced.

628
00:43:18,821 --> 00:43:20,760
And you'd have to get rid of all the waste.

629
00:43:20,761 --> 00:43:26,500
In the world that we live in, insects really do a lot of work for us for free.

630
00:43:26,501 --> 00:43:30,360
Exterminators now like to be called pest control professionals.

631
00:43:30,361 --> 00:43:35,580
The change is as much about political correctness as it is about a job description.

632
00:43:35,581 --> 00:43:40,140
In some ways, these professional assassins are caught between a bug and a hard place.

633
00:43:40,560 --> 00:43:44,360
If they kill everything in their path, they become environmental pariahs.

634
00:43:45,040 --> 00:43:50,120
But if they underachieve, allowing rodents and insects to crash the terrestrial party without controls,

635
00:43:50,420 --> 00:43:57,560
there is no doubt that the crawly barbarians at the gate will aggressively claim their share of our civilization.

